; The NAMED-PAYLOAD spelling of a nested `.cbor` — `inner = bytes .cbor uint` referenced as
; `bytes .cbor inner` — in top-level rule-BODY position. SUPPORTED: naming the inner payload is what
; gives it a boundary.
;
; A `.cbor` rule body mints a wrapper struct (the same one the `@newtype` spelling mints), so `Inner`
; is a real type with its own serialize fn and its own payload buffer, and `B` writes `42 41 05` for
; the value 5 — a byte string holding a byte string holding a uint (uint 5 = `05`; the inner byte
; string over that one byte = `41 05`; the outer byte string over those two = `42 41 05`; executed,
; not derived on paper). The reference crosses a TYPE rather than copying an encoding chain, so each
; level's staging buffer belongs to a different serialize fn — where the INLINE spelling
; (`bytes .cbor (bytes .cbor uint)`, tests/corpus/cbor_payload_inline_nested.cddl) has both levels in
; one fn and separates them by the depth suffix on every name instead.
;
; What this fixture pins, and the reason it is not a duplicate of that one, is the rule-body
; force-wrap seen from the OUTSIDE: were `inner` to register as a transparent alias again, its own
; `.cbor` would ride on the `RustType` a reference copies whole, the two spellings really would be
; one chain, and this file would stop testing a type boundary at all.
inner = bytes .cbor uint
b = bytes .cbor inner
